  "text": "Affix: A morpheme that is attached to another morpheme to modify its meaning, such as prefixes, suffixes, or infixes.\n\nAllophone: A variant form of a phoneme that occurs in a specific phonetic environment and is not distinguished in the language's sound system.\n\nAllophony: The occurrence of different allophones of the same phoneme in different phonetic environments.\n\nAssimilation: A process in which one sound becomes more like a neighboring sound, often due to articulatory ease.\n\nBack Chain: A process in which the phonological structure of a word is analyzed from the end to the beginning, typically used in phonological theory.\n\nBilingualism: The capacity to use two languages with equal or near-equal proficiency.\n\nCognitive Grammar: A set of theories in linguistics that focus on the mental representations of language structures.\n\nComplementarity: A relationship between two phonemes where they are never in the same phonetic environment.\n\nConsonant Cluster: A sequence of consonants that occur together without a vowel in between, often found in word-initial or word-final positions.\n\nConstructivist Approach: A method in language acquisition research that emphasizes the role of the learner in constructing linguistic knowledge.\n\nDative Alternation: A syntactic phenomenon in which a sentence can be expressed in two different ways, often with a change in the object's case or form.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound that is produced by a glide from one vowel to another within the same syllable.\n\nDissimilation: A process in which one sound becomes different from a neighboring sound, often to avoid harsh or difficult articulation.\n\nDative Shift: A transformation in sentence structure where the object of a transitive verb becomes the subject in an intransitive construction.\n\nDialect: A regional or social variation of a language that differs in vocabulary, grammar, and pronunciation.\n\nDol'nya: A dialect of the Tuvan language spoken in the Dol'na region of southern Siberia.\n\nEjective Consonant: A type of consonant produced with a burst of air from the mouth, often found in languages of the Americas and the Caucasus.\n\nElite Speech: The form of language associated with educated or socially prestigious speakers.\n\nEpenthesis: The insertion of a phoneme or syllable into a word to make it easier to pronounce or to satisfy phonotactic constraints.\n\nErosion: The loss of unstressed syllables or sounds in a word, often observed in speech or in some writing systems.\n\nEstuary: A transitional area where a river meets the sea, often used metaphorically in linguistics to describe language change or evolution.\n\nFiber: A bit of spoken language, typically from one to five syllables, used as a unit in linguistic analysis.\n\nFocalization: A process in communication where the speaker emphasizes a particular part of the message for clarity or effect.\n\nFricative: A consonant sound produced by restricting airflow through a narrow passage, resulting in friction, such as /f/ or /s/.\n\nFusional: A type of morphology where affixes can convey multiple grammatical categories, often found in Indo-European languages.\n\nGenitive: A case used to indicate possession or a relationship between two nouns.\n\nGrapheme: A single character in a writing system, such as \"a\" or \"th\" in English.\n\nHigh Register: A register of speech associated with formal or elevated topics and style.\n\nInfix: A morpheme inserted within a word, often in the middle of the word, such as \"un-happy\" in some languages.\n\nInterlanguage: The language system that a second language learner constructs, which may differ from the target language.\n\nInversion: A syntactic process in which the normal word order is reversed, often for emphasis or stylistic effect",
